  "preamble": "Depalatalization of a palatalized velar [kʲ] to plain [k] removes the secondary palatal articulation and typically merges the palatalized series with the plain velars. The best-known case is the 'centum' merger of Indo-European, in which the Proto-Indo-European palatovelar *ḱ (usually interpreted as [kʲ] or a fronted velar) fell together with plain *k in Italic, Greek, Celtic, Germanic and Tocharian. Even in the satem branches, Balto-Slavic shows conditioned depalatalization of *ḱ to k, as in Lithuanian akmuõ 'stone'. Hittite and Palaic merged *ḱ with *k (*ḱḗr > Hittite ker, *ḱéytor > Palaic kītār), whereas Luwian kept it apart as z; Melchert classes Anatolian as centum.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Depalatalization, Merger, Centumization",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Latin",
      "languageFamily": "Italic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "PIE *ḱm̥tóm 'hundred'",
          "to": "centum [ˈkentum]",
          "note": "The PIE palatovelar merged with plain *k in Proto-Italic (*kəntom), giving Latin initial /k/."
        }
      ]
    },
